00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:28.800
Tonight on Frontline, the Bloods of Nam.

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:50.680
From the network of public television stations, a presentation of KCTS Seattle, WNET New York, WPBT Miami, WTVS Detroit, and WGBH Boston.

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:55.760
This is Frontline with Judy Woodruff.

00:00:58.860 --> 00:00:59.220
Good evening.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.200
As Memorial Day approaches, we pause to remember our military veterans,

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:08.940
those who lost their lives or had them forever changed in war.

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:15.540
Tonight, some special remembrances from an overlooked group of veterans about an unpopular war.

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:22.120
This evening, war stories about what it was like to have been young, black, and in Vietnam.

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:26.740
A high percentage of the men on the front lines in Vietnam were black.

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:29.560
By most accounts, they had the highest casualties.

00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:34.780
They say they were fighting two wars, the enemy and discrimination.

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:40.200
But they also talk about battlefield brotherhood and lifelong friendships.

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:44.250
Our program is written and reported by Wallace Terry,

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:49.990
who covered the war for Time magazine and wrote a book on black soldiers in Vietnam.

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:52.630
Our producer is Wayne Ewing.

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:55.300
And now, The Bloods of Nam.

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:59.930
And I must warn you, this program contains graphic descriptions.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.780
of the horrors encountered in war.

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:28.980
Vietnam, a mean, dirty war.

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:34.110
It consumed American passions for 15 years.

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:38.880
It divided the nation more deeply than any other issue since the Civil War.

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:46.430
Black soldiers fought and died in Vietnam in disproportionate numbers.

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:49.980
That's why we call the front lines Soulville.

00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:53.290
But Vietnam was our first integrated war.

00:02:54.230 --> 00:02:59.120
It was a time when soldiers, black and white, became brothers against a common enemy.

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:03.680
If Vietnam had any redeeming value, it was this friendship.

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:10.890
But it was also a place of discrimination, where to gain your self-respect,

00:03:11.570 --> 00:03:13.470
you stood apart and called yourself a blood,

00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:20.120
a place where communist propaganda urged you to go home

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:22.540
instead of fighting another dark-skinned people.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:29.980
This is a film about what it was like.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:40.030
