00:19:22.910 --> 00:19:28.780
You take one man and hold up a whole battalion, you gotta spend $100,000 in rounds to kill him.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:33.660
It was a various occasion that we went out on a search and destroy mission.

00:19:34.040 --> 00:19:38.120
We ran into one North Vietnamese hardcore soldier.

00:19:38.770 --> 00:19:43.140
He was a regular, meaning that he was trained in North Vietnam.

00:19:43.560 --> 00:19:51.060
And a platoon of 30 to 35 men surrounded this one Vietnamese soldier in a tunnel.

00:19:52.080 --> 00:19:53.460
And we had interpreters.

00:19:53.620 --> 00:19:57.520
And we talked to him for about two hours trying to talk him out of the hole.

00:19:57.760 --> 00:19:59.980
And then we turned to our...

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:05.970
Our tactics of throwing guns into the tunnel, shooting into it, we fired everything in there.

00:20:06.210 --> 00:20:12.910
And we still use the interpreter to try to talk him out of it, but we spent two hours, 35 men against one.

00:20:13.570 --> 00:20:15.170
Took two hours to kill this man.

00:20:15.490 --> 00:20:29.330
And the only way that we killed him was that one of the men out of that platoon went in there with a shotgun and beat him to the draw.

00:20:30.480 --> 00:20:31.460
He would not give up.

00:20:32.720 --> 00:20:37.590
And this is the day that I really will remember in my life

00:20:38.190 --> 00:20:42.160
that if a man have a belief, a real belief,

00:20:44.060 --> 00:20:45.960
he should be willing to die for it.

00:20:47.020 --> 00:20:50.400
Now, I was proud of that North Vietnamese soldier

00:20:51.030 --> 00:20:54.890
because he gave me a living example of what a man is supposed to be.

00:20:56.660 --> 00:20:59.440
I like the Vietnamese. I just was very sorry.

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:05.810
do what we did there. But I didn't really dislike them. I thought they were very beautiful people.

00:21:06.030 --> 00:21:10.310
I thought they, you know, were real nice. Except for the ones who were standing in front of my

00:21:10.430 --> 00:21:15.780
front sight blades, you know, trying to hurt me. That person, we had problems. But basically,

00:21:15.800 --> 00:21:20.580
I liked the Vietnamese. I didn't really think of them as bad people, you know. But, you know,

00:21:20.660 --> 00:21:25.340
we had a war, and we were trying to save them from communism. Of course, you never realize when

00:21:25.340 --> 00:21:29.660
you go into the villages and watch them going through their changes as we walk through the

00:21:29.660 --> 00:21:29.880
village.

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:33.810
is that, you know, you wonder, do they know why we're here?

00:21:34.570 --> 00:21:37.630
When you were in Vietnam, do you think you looked at what was happening,

00:21:37.630 --> 00:21:40.190
at the people any differently because you were black?

00:21:40.450 --> 00:21:42.740
Yeah. What happened is, I think being black,

00:21:43.130 --> 00:21:47.500
you felt more affinity with people who had problems, who were in trouble.

00:21:47.910 --> 00:21:51.170
You felt more of an affinity with them because you had had trouble,

00:21:51.270 --> 00:21:52.510
and you had problems.

00:21:52.860 --> 00:21:56.960
I understand that during Vietnam War, Martin Luther King died.

00:21:57.220 --> 00:21:59.700
I want to know, did that bring up a lot of...

00:21:59.700 --> 00:21:59.740
Thank you.

00:22:00.410 --> 00:22:05.940
racial issues i happen to live in an all-black hut it was the first all-black hut in vietnam

00:22:05.940 --> 00:22:13.640
and there were eight of us in the hut and it was interesting that even before we knew that he had

00:22:13.640 --> 00:22:19.060
been killed all of a sudden these confederate flags started flying over a lot of the quonset

00:22:19.060 --> 00:22:26.030
huts and some of the major facilities and we kind of tried to figure out where our head was at like

00:22:26.030 --> 00:22:29.970
what do we do now you know do we take these folks on because we went to a couple of the huts

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:34.400
and asked them to take flags down because we knew what it seemed like they weren't up the day before

00:22:34.400 --> 00:22:39.730
they were up that day but i think it really hit us when over one of the warehouses i mean they had

00:22:39.730 --> 00:22:45.960
had to be about eight by ten foot flag confederate flag flying over the american flag we went to the

00:22:45.960 --> 00:22:51.510
ceo of the base and said look if you don't get these flags down there's going to be a problem

00:22:51.600 --> 00:22:59.510
a very serious problem within 15 minutes all the flags were down yes there was a problem in the

00:22:59.510 --> 00:22:59.870
most of

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:05.530
followed King's death. Black militancy swept across the rear areas. Bloods like Randolph

00:23:05.530 --> 00:23:10.890
Dobie organized themselves, raised their fists in black power salutes, studied black history,

00:23:11.370 --> 00:23:16.990
wore black beads and berets, and even chose to live in all black hoochies. Well, they had all

00:23:16.990 --> 00:23:23.270
white hoochies, and they did that primarily for commonality of interest. There was no

00:23:24.530 --> 00:23:29.620
assignment of hoochies. Basically, the whites were, or any group of...

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:36.460
marines could elect to stay in a certain hut and we went to the ceo and asked if we could have our

00:23:36.460 --> 00:23:43.720
hut so that we could have a hut of common interests and goals and relationships and the ceo granted us

00:23:43.720 --> 00:23:49.410
that privilege now how did the fellow your fellow white marines feel about this all-black hooch in

00:23:49.410 --> 00:23:55.850
their midst well at first they didn't seem to have any problems but later on there were a couple of

00:23:55.850 --> 00:23:59.970
incidences of cross burnings and they even tried to burn down the hat one.

00:24:00.280 --> 00:24:02.980
What do you mean, cross burnings? Did you see a cross burn in Vietnam?

00:24:03.550 --> 00:24:03.710
Yeah.

00:24:04.250 --> 00:24:04.470
Where?

00:24:04.930 --> 00:24:05.510
From the hut.

00:24:06.500 --> 00:24:10.400
What did you do when you saw a Confederate flag flying over a forward base?

00:24:10.620 --> 00:24:15.150
The damn thing came down, and the commander answered to me.

00:24:16.370 --> 00:24:17.450
Why did you bring it down?

00:24:18.960 --> 00:24:26.080
Because we weren't fighting the Civil War, and there's no such thing as northern blood or southern blood.

00:24:27.040 --> 00:24:29.960
There can be no such thing as black blood and white blood when you...

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:37.320
being fired at. And the interesting thing is, I never saw that happen in forward areas.

00:24:38.200 --> 00:24:43.140
It was only when you got to the relative security of the main base on the perimeter that you

00:24:43.140 --> 00:24:43.860
saw that happen.

00:24:45.360 --> 00:24:51.200
There was a amount of prejudice in Vietnam, but when you got out in the jungle, there

00:24:51.200 --> 00:24:56.660
was no room for prejudice, because you would want your enemy to see that other silhouette

00:24:56.660 --> 00:24:59.980
that they're standing beside you regardless of his color because he

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:04.980
got more targets to shoot at so this is what taught me that this is just a

00:25:04.980 --> 00:25:11.910
tradition that would just pass down to us to be prejudiced to look upon people

00:25:11.910 --> 00:25:16.430
differently because when your life is at stake it doesn't make any different

00:25:16.430 --> 00:25:19.830
that the man to the left or the right of you is black yellow red orange it makes

00:25:19.830 --> 00:25:25.190
no difference as long as he performed his duty and protects your back now this is

00:25:25.190 --> 00:25:29.790
what caused me to throw away, uh, to sit down and reanalyze.

00:25:30.250 --> 00:25:37.550
a lot of concepts that i had thoughts that i had you know especially when i was in vietnam i got

00:25:37.550 --> 00:25:46.640
you know very militant black power brother and everything but uh when i thought over my experience

00:25:46.640 --> 00:25:55.480
i came to the conclusion i would be worse than the perpetrators who are inflicting

00:25:56.910 --> 00:25:59.980
discrimination against me if I try to revert

00:26:00.310 --> 00:26:06.000
discrimination because i know better i know how it feels to be discriminated against i like to

00:26:06.000 --> 00:26:13.900
never seem consistent to you uh to grow up in a segregated society and then go off to the marines

00:26:13.900 --> 00:26:19.050
and defend it well you know you didn't really you see you see we always believed that the country

00:26:19.050 --> 00:26:23.370
was going to change you know you figure well these people are this way because they they don't

00:26:23.370 --> 00:26:28.770
know us they they don't really understand us as a people and you start like feel that maybe once

00:26:28.770 --> 00:26:29.790
They got to know us.

00:26:30.060 --> 00:26:34.220
and check out our values and see where we were coming from because i grew up with all of those

00:26:34.220 --> 00:26:42.680
traditional values love for home family and country and god god first i grew up with that

00:26:42.680 --> 00:26:47.880
you see as small as this town is we have three churches because religion was a very focal point

00:26:47.880 --> 00:26:52.790
in our community and we figured that well in time the country would believe in us they would see

00:26:52.790 --> 00:26:59.190
that we were good americans we were just as uh together as they were and it would be better well

00:26:59.190 --> 00:26:59.970
I found out

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:07.100
that that wasn't necessarily true in the marine corps the last segregated unit fought in korea i

00:27:07.100 --> 00:27:13.960
believe the year was 1950 where it was disbanded so from 50 to the to the 17-year period since you

00:27:14.690 --> 00:27:22.060
could you could say that pretty much complete integration had taken place um you found mixed

00:27:22.060 --> 00:27:28.180
units of course you found black officers white officers um in vietnam you found a variety of

00:27:28.180 --> 00:27:29.960
units, but there was no restriction.

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:33.000
Friction in the sense of blacks could do this or blacks could do that.

00:27:33.420 --> 00:27:35.420
It's fairly much integrated.

00:27:35.930 --> 00:27:39.330
You know, in Vietnam, we had our first company commanders

00:27:39.330 --> 00:27:43.610
and probably squadron commanders and company first sergeants

00:27:43.610 --> 00:27:48.370
and gave people an opportunity to get, you know, a tremendous amount of experience.

00:27:48.370 --> 00:27:52.900
It gave our comrades an opportunity to work with us

00:27:52.900 --> 00:27:57.340
and see that, you know, that the black American Marine could meet any task that anyone else was.

00:27:57.440 --> 00:27:58.680
In fact, there was just no difference.

00:27:59.060 --> 00:27:59.760
And...

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:03.040
Things will never be the same as it was prior to Vietnam.

00:28:04.050 --> 00:28:10.260
The majority of your ground units were composed to a significant number of minority troops.

00:28:10.720 --> 00:28:15.420
It was not unusual to find rifle companies that were over 50% black.

00:28:16.110 --> 00:28:17.110
And why would that be?

00:28:17.330 --> 00:28:24.100
Well, let's face it, as you come into the military, you're crafted into a skill according to various testings that are given to the individual.

00:28:25.120 --> 00:28:29.940
And with the Marine Corps especially, which is ground intensive, you'll find that the majority...

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:35.460
of those coming in would be sent to ground types of billets and when you're in the marine corps

00:28:35.460 --> 00:28:39.260
and you speak of ground you're speaking of a rifleman you're speaking of a grunt a foot soldier

00:28:39.980 --> 00:28:44.380
therefore the majority of minorities who came in were tested and placed in ground billets

00:28:44.380 --> 00:28:50.320
specifically riflemen and therefore you had in my opinion a much higher proportion of black

00:28:50.320 --> 00:28:56.740
casualties as opposed to the entire population the military didn't begin to integrate until after

00:28:56.740 --> 00:28:59.800
during and after the Korean War and Vietnam

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:05.750
war was the first war where there was an integrated fighting force in the history of america the the

00:29:05.750 --> 00:29:12.650
the integration part was your minority and your poor whites and hispanics that's who fought the

00:29:12.650 --> 00:29:22.980
war joe was one of my best friends in vietnam he was a white dude from georgia and he had a

00:29:22.980 --> 00:29:29.980
accent Joe used to walk point point in a military operation his

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:35.680
the first man that leads the company. Joe was walking maybe about the third man in front of me.

00:29:35.700 --> 00:29:40.720
I was about the third man behind Joe. And then as we were walking along a well-used trail by the

00:29:40.720 --> 00:29:46.940
enemy, Joe hit a booby trap which blew him up in the air, eight feet in the air. And he came back

00:29:46.940 --> 00:29:54.730
down and landed on his buttocks with his hand behind him in this position. And the booby trap

00:29:54.730 --> 00:29:58.660
had blown off his entire right leg across here.

00:30:00.030 --> 00:30:06.000
thing that was holding on his right leg was a piece of a piece of uh of joe flesh maybe about

00:30:06.000 --> 00:30:13.700
as thick as my thumb was holding his leg to his body and joe was semi-conscious but when joe began

00:30:13.700 --> 00:30:21.770
consciousness and raised up and saw his leg that he missed a whole leg he went into shock when this

00:30:21.770 --> 00:30:26.570
happened the medics were on him right away but joe never made it to the hospital because they

00:30:26.570 --> 00:30:29.870
say that he died from shock and at this time that when

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:34.710
Joe got hit. I couldn't go rush over there to Joe because usually you don't do things

00:30:34.710 --> 00:30:40.320
like that. I had to move in front of the trail and guard the trail and watch for the enemy

00:30:40.320 --> 00:30:45.960
so that the medevac, the medical helicopter, could come in and pick Joe up and take him

00:30:45.960 --> 00:30:51.240
back to the one in the rear hospital. And so all this time that I'm waiting for the

00:30:51.400 --> 00:30:59.200
helicopter to come out, I'm praying to God, please let me see the enemy. Lord, please

00:30:59.200 --> 00:30:59.980
Please let me see anything.

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:04.720
one and then it got so bad that i was standing there waiting watching that trail i said lord

00:31:04.720 --> 00:31:13.320
anybody a woman a child anybody i just wanted to kill someone because i lost a real friend and

00:31:13.320 --> 00:31:20.100
i've i've carried this with me from that day until now i will never ever forget joe although joe's

00:31:20.100 --> 00:31:26.530
family might forget him his wife and his child might forget him but i will never ever forget joe

00:31:28.410 --> 00:31:29.970
I was of a pilot of

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:39.210
a F-105 Thunder Chief fighter, fighter bomb. The target that day was a surface-to-air missile

00:31:39.210 --> 00:31:45.340
installation just northeast of Hanoi and I was leading a flight of four Thunder Chiefs on that

00:31:45.340 --> 00:31:55.700
target on 22nd of October 1965. Just prior to the target I realized I'd been hit and to back that up

00:31:55.700 --> 00:31:59.340
I had been flying at treetop level for 34 minutes.

00:32:00.700 --> 00:32:07.500
at better than 500 miles an hour all the way and about two minutes after i delivered my weapons

00:32:07.500 --> 00:32:15.520
on the target the aircraft exploded fred cherry was lucky to land in one piece bullets whizzed by

00:32:15.520 --> 00:32:21.420
his parachute his shoulder was smashed and his wrist was broken he was the 43rd american captured

00:32:21.420 --> 00:32:27.040
in the north and the first block the communists tied him up and took him to a schoolyard where

00:32:27.040 --> 00:32:29.840
villagers poured out shouting, kill the Yankee.

00:32:30.350 --> 00:32:35.750
The first thing I can remember saying is, looking around, I says, damn, I'll be here a long time.

00:32:35.890 --> 00:32:42.130
But that long time to me at that point in time was three, four months max,

00:32:42.850 --> 00:32:45.770
because we're going to get this thing over with and we'll be back home.

00:32:46.990 --> 00:32:51.150
Well, that three to four months, that long time I consider that time,

00:32:52.420 --> 00:32:54.160
turning to be seven and a half years.

00:32:54.760 --> 00:32:59.920
A few days before, a young Navy pilot from Tennessee took off for North Vietnam.

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:01.560
from the carrier independence.

00:33:02.260 --> 00:33:04.280
Porter Halliburton and Fred Cherry

00:33:04.280 --> 00:33:07.500
would soon find their lives forever intertwined.

00:33:07.740 --> 00:33:08.780
I was one of these guys

00:33:08.780 --> 00:33:10.320
that just never thought too much

00:33:10.320 --> 00:33:11.460
about being captured.

00:33:11.700 --> 00:33:13.640
I thought, well, maybe I would get killed,

00:33:14.280 --> 00:33:17.280
but never captured.

00:33:17.600 --> 00:33:19.280
I think the Vietnamese

00:33:19.280 --> 00:33:24.100
had learned enough about Halley.

00:33:25.520 --> 00:33:27.800
I know he was a Navy aviator.

00:33:28.080 --> 00:33:29.930
I am an Air Force aviator.

00:33:30.500 --> 00:33:35.650
I'm a southern black, he's a southern white, he's younger than I.

00:33:36.000 --> 00:33:41.160
I don't know whether they thought moving me in with Fred was going to be the ultimate horror or not,

00:33:41.530 --> 00:33:46.130
the thing that would break me, but there was no reason for it.

00:33:46.300 --> 00:33:50.760
I had not suddenly decided to talk to them, I hadn't done anything,

00:33:51.060 --> 00:33:54.220
and they had not threatened me with that specifically.

00:33:55.440 --> 00:33:59.960
The communists wanted Fred to broadcast orders to the Bloods fighting in the South to give up the...

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:07.640
war it would be a propaganda coup so they tortured him i spent a total of 702 days in solitary

00:34:07.640 --> 00:34:16.270
confinement well i think the stakes were basically his honor um i don't think that he could have

00:34:17.480 --> 00:34:21.960
lived with himself you know if he had if he had done that if he cooperated with him

00:34:22.080 --> 00:34:29.880
and with them i think he could have secured for himself much better treatment um perhaps

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:36.400
even early release because certainly being the first black there I mean he

00:34:36.400 --> 00:34:40.820
was could be somewhat of a celebrity certainly would generate a lot of

00:34:41.100 --> 00:34:45.440
interest in the press and so on and if he'd been cooperative then of course

00:34:45.440 --> 00:34:48.960
they would have treated him very well and I think they may have considered

00:34:48.960 --> 00:34:58.020
releasing him early one period then 67 93 days straight that included beatings

00:34:58.020 --> 00:34:59.800
three times a day

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:04.900
or more. I think the guards were obligated to do it at least three times and if they

00:35:04.900 --> 00:35:10.860
wanted to throw something in extra that they could do that too. And that is living in leg

00:35:10.860 --> 00:35:18.000
irons and the beatings and almost half the time when you reach down to pick up your bowl

00:35:18.000 --> 00:35:25.020
of soup, water soup, that was knocked over on you. And it's summertime and it's hot and there's ants

00:35:25.020 --> 00:35:29.840
and their flies, and I never felt at any point

00:35:30.060 --> 00:35:33.700
that it would be better if Fred gave in to him,

00:35:33.980 --> 00:35:35.640
even if he got medical treatment.

00:35:35.900 --> 00:35:41.310
How they did everything one can do to keep one alive.

00:35:41.430 --> 00:35:45.510
That was bathe me, feed me, even his food,

00:35:45.630 --> 00:35:48.710
if I could handle it.

00:35:49.810 --> 00:35:51.290
Most times I didn't want mine.

00:35:51.610 --> 00:35:54.070
I counted up one day, and he had something like

00:35:54.070 --> 00:35:58.140
nine major open wounds in his body at one time.

00:35:58.650 --> 00:35:59.850
That's not counting.

00:36:00.380 --> 00:36:06.320
the one on his shoulder so it's pretty tough watching him even though he didn't complain

00:36:06.320 --> 00:36:19.440
very often they came in they sued up he was moving couldn't believe it i you know good lord i lost

00:36:19.820 --> 00:36:29.560
everything if i lose halley but they took him and uh away and i was left it was at night that's the

00:36:29.560 --> 00:36:29.960
the most long

00:36:31.660 --> 00:36:37.710
I knew it was not going to be good, and I was really heartbroken.

00:36:38.680 --> 00:36:38.920
Why?

00:36:40.220 --> 00:36:41.680
Because I'd grown to love him.

00:36:43.620 --> 00:36:47.060
They were standing right there when they told him to suit up and let's go,

00:36:48.340 --> 00:36:53.400
because in spite of what the hell they said, we were going to say goodbye

00:36:53.400 --> 00:36:59.090
because we were both shedding tears, but they took him.

00:37:02.720 --> 00:37:07.180
It would be over six years before Porter Halliburton and Fred Cherry would see each other again.

00:37:08.180 --> 00:37:10.620
That's when they came home with the other POWs.

00:37:11.540 --> 00:37:14.260
But they were the only ones who got a hero's welcome.

00:37:14.480 --> 00:37:22.770
When you come back, everybody, they give you, was it, 22 hours?

00:37:23.240 --> 00:37:23.940
Get back here.

00:37:24.840 --> 00:37:29.320
And you have to make a crazy adjustment because you...

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:34.720
in a killing situation now you're back in what they call back in the world you have to be your

00:37:34.720 --> 00:37:40.120
own civilized now no debriefing or whatever word they want to call that you know i don't know if

00:37:40.120 --> 00:37:45.730
you can understand the kind of psychological change that you have to change into for the most

00:37:45.730 --> 00:37:53.330
part we were looked upon as baby killers drug addicts malcontents and and any other kind of

00:37:53.330 --> 00:37:59.920
negative uh concept that you have we we but we're boiling uh we

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:04.740
We weren't accepted by our own peers for the most part because they were protesting against the war.

00:38:05.880 --> 00:38:09.540
The older folks didn't accept us because they said we were losing the war.

00:38:10.120 --> 00:38:15.420
As a black serviceman coming home, one to be ostracized for having participated in the Vietnam War,

00:38:15.640 --> 00:38:22.920
and then to return to a society that still was not fully accepting of a black's capabilities, would be a double hurt.

00:38:23.180 --> 00:38:29.840
And I think that in many instances, the reaction on the part of the black veteran was to...

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:36.340
become violent in terms of demanding what was felt to be his due but in other cases uh those

00:38:36.340 --> 00:38:43.620
not mentally strong enough uh took other paths and uh it was tragic and it still is it's not over yet

00:38:43.620 --> 00:38:49.850
you know gene uh when the guys came home from world war ii you know they came home with same

00:38:50.290 --> 00:38:56.100
units uh two or three weeks on the troop ship when they got here the bands were playing

00:38:56.100 --> 00:38:57.080
And Vietnam was all different.

00:38:57.300 --> 00:38:59.090
You guys came home one at a time.

00:38:59.150 --> 00:38:59.770
I didn't come home.

00:39:01.200 --> 00:39:10.970
